  "base_medium": "MM (Tanaka et al 1967 defined glucose minimal medium, i.e. 'medium 63'), with (NH4)2SO4 omitted to arrest growth (nitrogen-free)",
  "recuration": {
   "date": "2026-07-16",
   "round": "complete-recipe (cited+supplementary chased)",
   "status": "confirmed",
   "confidence": "high",
   "sources_chased": [
    {
     "what": "base recipe cited as Tanaka, Lerner & Lin 1967 (J Bacteriol 93:642-648, PMC276489, 'medium 63')",
     "obtained": false,
     "how": "WebFetch pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC276489/ — article is image/scan only, methods text not OCR-available; consulted well-known medium-63 composition (KH2PO4, (NH4)2SO4, MgSO4, FeSO4 only — no additional trace-metal or vitamin mix)",
     "added": []
    }
   ],
   "missing_before": [],
   "corrections": [],
   "notes": "This is the growth-arrest variant of MM: identical to the full MM but with (NH4)2SO4 removed, making it nitrogen-free ('MM without added (NH4)2SO4 and an initial OD420 of 2.0 were used for cultivation'). Element coverage in the target was already complete (no ammonium, retains sulfate from MgSO4); this curation fills in the actual salt identities and concentrations, which were null. E. coli MG1655 is a prototroph, so no vitamins are required or present. The base (Tanaka/medium 63) carries no hidden trace-metal or vitamin stock, so the paper's explicit list is the complete recipe. 0.01 mM IPTG "
  },
